Title: RUNBOOK measurements are undeclared in the figure guard
Created: 2026-08-29
Author: claude

--- log ---
2026-08-29T11:20Z claude created

--- body ---
## Context

Found by the /roar sweep after PR #26. `/gaze` had just caught the same class
one document over: `DECISIONS.md` was absent from `bench/check_figures.py`'s
`PROSE` map, so a figure written into it drifted onto a superseded run within a
day and nothing could see it. Fixing that instance prompted the question of
which OTHER prose documents quote measurements without declaring them.

`RUNBOOK.md` does, in five places, and it is not in the map:

- line 36 — `warm p95 1 773,0 ms stopword-less against 392,3 ms on stock`
- line 54 — `263,7 s and 1 755,6 MiB on doudou`
- line 116 — `reproduces 0011's 2 084,9 MiB class`
- line 171 — `a number that would read "1 773,0 ms"`

These are this repo's own measurements, from `bench/results/`, quoted in prose.
CLAUDE.md's rule is unconditional: *every measurement quoted in prose is
declared there with an anchor; when you re-measure, the guard tells you every
prose site to update.* RUNBOOK's sites are exactly the ones it would not tell
you about.

`CONSTRAINTS.md` was checked and quotes none — it is correctly absent.
`FIELD-REVIEW.md` carries 24 measurement-shaped figures but they are OTHER
projects' claimed numbers, not ours, and the guard has nothing to compare them
against; it is out of scope here and is the reason 0161 asks for a rule that
distinguishes the two rather than a grep that cannot.

## Why this matters more than the count suggests

RUNBOOK is an instruction document: its numbers are what an operator compares a
fresh run against. A stale figure there does not merely misinform, it makes a
correct run look like a regression, or a regression look correct.

`1 773,0 ms` and `392,3 ms` are X2's verdict pair, and X2 was re-measured on
2026-08-29 (0025's log). Whether these two survived that re-measurement is
exactly the question the guard exists to answer mechanically, and here it cannot.

## Actions

1. Add `"runbook": ["RUNBOOK.md"]` to `PROSE` in `bench/check_figures.py`.
2. Declare each of the four distinct figures against its artifact in
   `bench/results/0025-x2-stopwordless/` and `bench/results/0011-rss/`.
3. Where a figure has no artifact behind it, say so in the ticket rather than
   inventing a declaration — an undeclarable number in an instruction document
   is its own finding.

## Test

Red step: add the declarations, run `python3 bench/check_figures.py`, and watch
it report STALE for any RUNBOOK figure that no longer matches its artifact.
Then perturb one deliberately and confirm it is caught before restoring — an
all-clear never seen to fail is not evidence.

## Verification

- [ ] `python3 bench/check_figures.py` reports 0 stale with RUNBOOK covered
- [ ] the pair count rises by the number of declarations added
- [ ] a planted wrong value in RUNBOOK.md produces STALE, then restores clean

## Invariants

- `make check` stays green.
- No figure is edited to match an artifact without checking which of the two is
  right: a guard failure can mean the prose drifted OR the artifact was
  regenerated on a different substrate.

## Exit criteria

- RUNBOOK.md is in `PROSE` and every measurement it quotes is declared, or
  recorded as undeclarable with the reason.
